comments[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-15T07:10:58.232Z","harness:claude-code","Design decision (2026-11-15), recorded before implementation: the one-call sweep in criterion 3 is delivered through the list grammar that already exists, not through two new list flags.\n\n- Ownership is keyed on `assignee` alone. The item-store ownership guard reads `claim_principal` only when an assignee is present, and both claim and release write the two fields together. So \"no assignee and no live claim\" is exactly `--assignee-filter unassigned`.\n- Every item mutation bumps `updated_at`. Measured on a sandbox tracker: create, comment_add, note_add, files_add and claim each moved it. So \"no update within N hours\" is exactly `--updated-before -<N>h`. The relative form is already accepted (`^[+-]?\\d+[hdwm]$`).\n- `pm list --status in_progress --assignee-filter unassigned --updated-before -72h` therefore selects stale unowned work in one call today. Adding `--unowned` and `--idle` would add two canonical flags to each of the eight list commands, whose flag budgets are ratcheted at zero headroom under the one-screen grammar milestone, for no new selection power.\n- What was actually missing is discoverability. Health and the remediation registry now publish these exact commands as the structured remedy, using the threshold that the workspace configured.\n\nThe acceptance criteria are updated to that form. Health keeps the `stale_in_progress_items` advisory unchanged and adds `in_progress_unowned` (independent of idleness) and `in_progress_idle` (independent of ownership).\n"
  "2026-11-15T08:34:42.418Z","harness:claude-code","Verification evidence (2026-11-15, branch feat/stale-work-visibility-and-takeover):\n\n- **Shared scan.** `scanInProgressWork` and `inspectInProgressWork` classify in-progress work into `unowned`, `idle` and `stale` from one activity clock. History is read only for items whose `updated_at` already looks idle or is unparseable. `stale_in_progress_items` keeps its exact population.\n- **Health.** `in_progress_unowned:<n>` and `in_progress_idle:<n>` are advisory (`ok` unchanged), live under `details.in_progress_work` with bounded ids and sweeps, and appear in `remediation_map` with the configured status and threshold. A sandbox run with the threshold at 48 shows `pm list --status in_progress --updated-before -48h`. The registry now also covers `stale_in_progress_items`.\n- **Context.** The summary carries `in_progress_unowned` and `in_progress_idle` next to `in_progress`, only when non-zero (absent when every in-progress item is held and active).\n- **Linked tests.** `pm test pm-gh1724 --run` passed (run test-local-mwhkuzp5-lrc3ay).\n- **Full suite.** 12,808 tests passed. The only failures were the token-budget pin and an SDK snapshot refresh, both fixed. Exact 100% coverage still needs a final run on the exact head, which follows below.\n- **Token-budget fixture.** Its in-progress child is now owned by the fixture author, so the documented `health --check-only` ceiling of 164 tokens holds (measured 159). An unowned in-progress child is itself a GH-1724 advisory.\n- **Calibration.** The context-intent calibration re-approved one change: the scale-tier context summary gained 22 bytes for `in_progress_idle`.\n"
  "2026-11-15T09:03:19.643Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 1 on PR #1734 changed the shared activity clock this item's health and context counts use: it is now updated_at itself, with the newest history entry as a fallback only when updated_at does not parse. The printed --updated-before sweeps therefore list exactly the advisories' population. Custom status ids are now shell-quoted in every remedy. Details and the other three findings are on pm-gh1336."
